    <!-- ===== STEP 4: RECOVERY ===== -->
    <section id="recovery">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-header">
          <div class="step-pill">Step 4</div>
          <h2>Recovery & The 3-Month Rule</h2>
          <p class="section-sub">Cumulative lifetime exposure and dose per session are the primary risk factors. Frequency matters, but there is no single "safe interval" established by research.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="recovery-grid">
          <div class="recovery-card recovery-main">
            <h3>Use Spacing: What the Evidence Actually Says</h3>
            <p>The "3-month rule" originates from <strong>Ann Shulgin's personal clinical observation</strong>, not a controlled study. But that doesn't mean spacing is arbitrary, it means the <em>number</em> wasn't derived from research. The biology behind spacing matters, and it involves three distinct processes:</p>
            <div class="recovery-reasons">
              <div class="recovery-reason">
                <h4>Why Spacing Matters (The Layered Mechanism)</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Days–weeks:</strong> 5-HT2A receptor downregulation, the postsynaptic receptors desensitize after being flooded with serotonin. This is the primary driver of "losing the magic" in occasional users and recovers over weeks</li>
                  <li><strong>Weeks:</strong> Depleted releasable serotonin pool, the presynaptic terminals release less serotonin until vesicle stores replenish. Also recovers with adequate rest</li>
                  <li><strong>Months–years (heavy use only):</strong> Structural SERT reduction, axon terminal degeneration documented in neuroimaging. This is what the 6–18 month recovery timelines describe, but those studies enrolled users with a median of 50–244 lifetime sessions (Erritzoe 2011; Selvaraj 2009). Occasional users fall entirely outside those study populations</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="recovery-reason">
                <h4>What Actually Drives Risk</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Total lifetime doses</strong>, the single biggest predictor of SERT changes in human neuroimaging studies</li>
                  <li><strong>Dose per session</strong>, neurotoxicity is strongly dose-dependent; lower doses reduce risk</li>
                  <li><strong>Hot environments</strong>, hyperthermia dramatically amplifies oxidative damage to serotonin terminals</li>
                  <li><strong>Binge re-dosing</strong>, multiple doses within a session compounds both depletion and oxidative stress</li>
                  <li><strong>Individual genetics</strong>, SERT and MAO-A variants affect serotonin turnover and vulnerability</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="note-text"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> 3-month spacing is a reasonable conservative heuristic, but it's not derived from a specific study. The MAPS Phase 3 trials used ~4-week intervals in a limited, supervised clinical context, 3 sessions total. "Losing the magic" in occasional users is primarily receptor desensitization (recovers in weeks), not the structural SERT damage documented only in heavy users. The real levers are fewer total lifetime uses, lower doses per session, staying cool, and avoiding re-dosing within a single session.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="recovery-card">
            <h3>The "Comedown" Window</h3>
            <p>The days following MDMA use are characterized by reduced serotonin availability. Common experiences:</p>
            <div class="timeline-events">
              <div class="event">
                <span class="event-time">Day 1–2</span>
                <span class="event-desc">Fatigue, reduced mood, physical recovery</span>
              </div>
              <div class="event">
                <span class="event-time">Day 3–7</span>
                <span class="event-desc">"Tuesday Blues", lower serotonin, possible low mood</span>
              </div>
              <div class="event">
                <span class="event-time">Week 2–4</span>
                <span class="event-desc">Gradual serotonin recovery for most users</span>
              </div>
              <div class="event">
                <span class="event-time">Month 1–3+</span>
                <span class="event-desc">Subjective baseline returns for most occasional users; SERT structural recovery takes longer and depends on cumulative dose history</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="note-text">Severity varies widely by individual, dose, frequency, and genetics. Some people experience minimal comedowns; others experience significant depression lasting weeks.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== RESEARCH ===== -->
    <section id="research">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Research & Evidence Base</h2>
          <p class="section-sub">The peer-reviewed science behind harm reduction recommendations. Links open PubMed or source publications.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="research-grid">

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-neuro">Neurotoxicity</span>
            <h4>MDMA and Serotonin Transporter Density</h4>
            <p>A comprehensive review of 25 years of human MDMA research found significant associations between recreational MDMA use and reductions in serotonin transporter (SERT) density, with dose-dependent relationships in human neuroimaging studies.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23836598/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Parrott AC (2013). Human Psychobiology of MDMA, PubMed →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-thermo">Hyperthermia</span>
            <h4>Temperature as the Primary Neurotoxic Amplifier</h4>
            <p>Studies in non-human primates demonstrated that maintaining normothermia (normal body temperature) during MDMA administration nearly abolished the serotonergic neurotoxicity observed in hyperthermic conditions, even at high doses. Temperature management is the most evidence-based harm reduction intervention.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10592229/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Dafters et al. (1999). Body temperature and MDMA-induced serotonin neurotoxicity, PubMed →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-antioxidant">Antioxidants</span>
            <h4>Antioxidant Neuroprotection: Mechanistic Evidence</h4>
            <p>MDMA neurotoxicity is substantially mediated by reactive oxygen species formed during serotonin metabolism and dopamine oxidation inside serotonin terminals. Multiple animal studies show specific antioxidants can prevent this damage: alpha-lipoic acid (Aguirre et al., 1999) and Vitamin C (Shankaran &amp; Gudelsky, 2001) fully prevented serotonin depletion in rats. ALCAR protected mitochondrial integrity. NAC, as a glutathione precursor, protected hippocampal structure and memory. No human controlled trials exist, all evidence is preclinical, but the mechanism is well-characterized.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10619665/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Aguirre et al. (1999). Alpha-lipoic acid prevents MDMA neurotoxicity, Neuropharmacolology →
            </a>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11170222/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link" style="margin-top:6px; display:block;">
              Shankaran &amp; Gudelsky (2001). Vitamin C prevents MDMA-induced hydroxyl radicals and 5-HT loss, Synapse →
            </a>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s11011-014-9598-0" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link" style="margin-top:6px; display:block;">
              Metabolic Brain Disease (2015). NAC attenuation of MDMA neurotoxicity →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-clinical">Clinical Research</span>
            <h4>MDMA-Assisted Therapy for PTSD</h4>
            <p>MAPS-sponsored Phase 3 clinical trials demonstrated that MDMA-assisted psychotherapy produced substantial reductions in PTSD symptoms in treatment-resistant patients. These trials establish MDMA's therapeutic potential under controlled, temperature-managed conditions with precise dosing, further illustrating how risk is context-dependent.</p>
            <a href="https://maps.org/mdma/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              MAPS Clinical Research Program →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-hydration">Hydration</span>
            <h4>Hyponatremia: Deaths from Over-Hydration</h4>
            <p>Case series have documented MDMA-associated deaths from hyponatremia (dilutional low sodium) caused by excessive water ingestion combined with MDMA-stimulated ADH secretion. Affected individuals drank large volumes of plain water without electrolyte replacement, illustrating that "drink lots of water" is dangerous advice without context.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8639584/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Henry et al. (1996). Ecstasy and water intoxication (hyponatremia), BMJ case series →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-antioxidant">Bruxism</span>
            <h4>Magnesium and MDMA-Induced Jaw Clenching</h4>
            <p>MDMA-induced bruxism (jaw clenching and teeth grinding) is driven by excessive dopaminergic activation in basal ganglia motor circuitry, the same pathways targeted in movement disorder research. Magnesium acts as a natural NMDA receptor antagonist and modulates voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing the excitatory signaling that causes involuntary jaw contractions. Research on bruxism mechanisms and magnesium's neuromodulatory role supports this approach. Epidemiological studies also link low magnesium levels to elevated bruxism incidence in the general population.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29986691/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Lobbezoo et al. (2018). Bruxism defined and graded: International consensus, mechanisms overview, PubMed →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-antioxidant">5-HTP / EGCG</span>
            <h4>EGCG Inhibits Peripheral 5-HTP Conversion</h4>
            <p>When 5-HTP is taken without a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor, a significant fraction is converted to serotonin in the gut and peripheral tissues by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (AADC) before reaching the brain. This peripheral serotonin cannot cross the blood-brain barrier and produces GI and cardiovascular effects without replenishing central serotonin. EGCG (epigallocatechin gallate), the principal catechin in green tea extract, inhibits peripheral AADC activity, increasing the proportion of 5-HTP available for central serotonin synthesis. This pairing is the basis of the post-MDMA recovery protocol used in harm reduction communities worldwide.</p>
            <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2556089/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              Shen &amp; Bhagwat (1991). Catechin inhibition of AADC and peripheral 5-HTP conversion, Biochemical Pharmacology, PubMed →
            </a>
          </div>

          <div class="research-card">
            <span class="research-tag tag-supply">Drug Supply</span>
            <h4>Fentanyl in the Stimulant Supply</h4>
            <p>DEA and CDC surveillance data document increasing fentanyl detection in non-opioid drug supplies, including stimulants and MDMA. Drug checking services report contamination in a meaningful proportion of samples. This fundamentally changes the risk calculus of any unverified substance.</p>
            <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/deaths/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="research-link">
              CDC Drug Overdose Surveillance →
            </a>
          </div>

        </div>

